							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;"><div class="quoteheader">Quote</div><div class="quote">Just for another perspective on the issue, we had this cat at Highline Community College who was part of the NOI and was actually pretty amiable</div><br />here&#039;s my two cents on the issue....<br /><br />when i still lived in baltimore, i used to work for DOC records, wich was owned and operated by 3 NOI brothers. they were deep on the family tip and most of the heads who worked/performed for the label were FOI young cats from west baltimore (you know, where THE WIRE is filmed..)<br />anyway, they were the first real non-christian/judaic people i ever really got to know on a personal level, and they made a big influence on me. they were the first cats i ever met thta were my age that seemed really, really mature on some knowledge level steez and they were extremely down to constantly try and educate me just by setting a good example in righteousness of thought and action. they were non-pushy or militant, and they totally accepted me as an outsider based on the fact im also a person of color and just due to the fact i didnt try to front on them. i remember the mixing engineer at launchpad studios where they did all thier albums was a white guy.<br /><br />anyway, the best way to understand the NOI/FOI is probably to just try and meet someone at a community center or talk to someone at a mosque. i dont know what its like out here, but theres noi centers all over the place in DC and baltimore and usually a lot of the heads who organize behind the scenes at grassroots hiphop activist events are muslim or reformed.<br /><br />and for the record, ive eaten a thousand beanpies, and they all sucked.</div>
						</td>
					</tr>
