						<td valign="top" width="85%" height="100%">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=484.msg8318#msg8318"><img src="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/Themes/safgrey105/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=484.msg8318#msg8318">Re: the nation of islam, the good and the bad</a></b>
									<div class="smalltext">&#171; <b>Reply #35 on:</b> December 13, 2006, 11:13:42 am &#187;</div></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;">Just Beasy was pretty much right on as to what I said.&nbsp; I guess when I said &quot;spit in my face&quot; I wasn&#039;t really meaning that they&#039;d spit in my face literaly... but more that the sentiment of it would be there.&nbsp; One thing: as a white man who converted to Islam, some would view it just like how a lot of people hate wiggers or backpackers.&nbsp; ...the white man emulating and taking over a culture... or as a post 9-11 narc trying to bust the neo-commie that is Islam.&nbsp; Or possibly I&#039;m just a white devil speaking swine and trying to poison their minds with what I&#039;m saying.&nbsp; <br /><br />But... like other people said the NOI is like comparing Mormans to Christians.  I remember being in the Misjid and when ever somebody would mention Farakhan or the Nation, everybody would roll their eyes like, &quot;what a bunch of wackos.&quot;<br /><br />The point I was just making was that if I walked up there in my kufi and thobe, (when I was practicing) and just said salaam or what&#039;s up, they wouldn&#039;t give me the time of day.<br /><br />One thing I find interesting is directly across the street from the NOI is the Gambian International, (a muslim goods shop.)  Complete and total polar opposites of the spectrium side by side.  But... I bet the brothers at the NOI are just as nice, supportive and a community as the guys at the Gambian store.  But, alas I will never know.<br /><br />Serriously though, that store is dope and the guys there are really nice if your looking for something.  (kufis, thobes, prayer mats, books, saawak)  I don&#039;t know if they have haalal meats, but there&#039;s a joint up the street on... I think Rainier and 23rd.</div>
						</td>
