							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=484.msg8525#msg8525"><img src="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/Themes/safgrey105/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=484.msg8525#msg8525">Re: the nation of islam, the good and the bad</a></b>
									<div class="smalltext">&#171; <b>Reply #36 on:</b> December 14, 2006, 08:45:07 am &#187;</div></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;">Okay... people have been p.m.ing me as to my practicing or lack there of with Islam.&nbsp; So here is my reasons.<br />-----------------------------------------------------<br />Technically, I will always be muslim... just because the word muslim means to submit to God.<br /><br />But... as for why I&#039;m not practicing.&nbsp; Many reasons, but they have nothing to do with the current state of racisim, fear and hate that has emerged post 9-11.<br /><br />To boil it down the main reason I quit practicing had to do with my relationship with God.&nbsp; When I was muslim I found that there was more of a focus on the practice of the religion than with my realtionship with God.&nbsp; Due to that I was finding my spirituality was hollow.&nbsp; I loved the people, I loved the culture, I loved salaat, the call to prayer, I loved going to the misjid and sitting with my brothers... but I didn&#039;t feel any connection with myself and God.&nbsp; There was more of a focus on reciting a prayer correctly than with me being able to actually talk to God, (so to speak.)<br /><br />Now this is just my opinion and just reflects my experiences with Islam.&nbsp; I&#039;m pretty sure you will find people that feel just the opposite and have a wonderful relationship with God through Islam.&nbsp; But... I will tell you that I honestly miss going to the Mosque and meeting with my brothers, fasting, debating etc.&nbsp; It will ALWAYS be a part of me, hence my name... Dawhud, (daa-Wu&#039;d) that&#039;s David in Arabic and obviously it&#039;s my avitar.&nbsp; I&#039;ve also got it written in Arabic on my back... kind of a reminder of my past and what has made me who I am.<br /><br />...like they say, &quot;All roads lead to Mecca.&quot;&nbsp; I&#039;m just a traveling man like Mos Def.</div>
						</td>
					</tr>
